    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the state finance law, the general municipal law, the public\nauthorities law and the highway law, in relation to enacting the New York\nstate buy American salt act",
    "basePrintNo": "S9441",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9441-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 50,
       "fullName": "John Mannion",
       "imgName": "1315_John_Mannion.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1498,
       "sessionMemberId": 1854,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "MANNION"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §162-a, St Fin L; add §104-d, Gen Muni L; add §2877-a, Pub Auth L; add §12-a, Hway L",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9441\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the state finance law, the general municipal law, the\npublic authorities law and the highway law, in relation to enacting the\nNew York state buy American salt act\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends the title of the bill as the \"New York state buy\nAmerican salt act.\"\n\nSection two amends the state finance law by adding a new Section 162a to\nprovide that any department or agency purchasing rock salt must purchase\nAmerican mined or hand harvested salt, subject to waiver and other\nprovisions.\n\nSection three amends the general municipal law by adding a new section\n104d to provide that any department or agency of a political subdivision\npurchasing rock salt must purchase American mined or hand harvested\nsalt, subject to waiver and other provisions.\n\nSection four amends the public authority's law by adding a new section\n2877a to provide that any public authority within the state must\npurchase American mined or hand harvested salt, subject to waiver and\nother provisions.\n\nSection five amends the highway law by adding a new section 12a to\nprovide that any public authority purchasing rock salt must purchase\nAmerican mined or hand harvested salt, subject to waiver and other\nprovisions.\n\nSection six contains the severability clause. Section seven sets the\neffective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York State has at least two salt mines in the state that provide\nrock salt for road deicing purposes, with these two large mines located\nin Upstate New York. Despite these mines offering good paying jobs for\nworkers, competition with foreign salt operations has recently harmed\neach mine. For example in 2019, a mine near Rochester laid off 260\nemployees when non-US companies were awarded over $18.8 million in state\ncontracts, representing about 7% of its overall business. Stories of\nsalt mined in Egypt sitting in ports along the Hudson River have become\ncommon, despite our state and country having operational salt mines here\nin the United States.\n\nSalt mined or harvested in New York State adds an estimated $3.00 in tax\nrevenue for every ton of salt produced, providing a financial boon to\nthe state and various counties, in addition to employing workers, clear-\nly showing the fiscal and economic benefits to purchasing material\nproduced in the state. New York has previously prioritized the purchase\nof goods by focusing on a buy American strategy, most recently in 2017\nwhen the Governor and Legislature agreed to prioritize American made\nsteel in state contracts. While purchasing American products does not\nnecessarily Team New York products will be purchased, it does present a\nlegal framework that still gives New York mines an advantage over\nforeign competition, and ample opportunity exists in mines outside of\nNew York, including in Ohio and Louisiana, to make up much of the\ndifferent in salt products needed.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-2020: S.8966 Referred to Rules\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
